WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.730
If there's one thing that movie fans everywhere
seem to be able to agree on, it's that Keanu

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:07.000
Reeves is a pretty awesome guy.

00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:12.660
But although his career is going great nowadays,
Reeves once spent over a decade blacklisted

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:14.400
by one of Hollywood's biggest studios.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.720
So just why was he thrown into "movie jail"
for 11 long years?

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.760
Before you can really understand why Keanu
Reeves spent so long out in the wilderness,

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.120
you've first got to understand the events
leading up to his exile.

00:00:27.580 --> 00:00:30.140
And for that, you've got to start at the beginning
of his career.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:35.100
After featuring opposite Rob Lowe in a forgettable
yet strangely successful hockey movie called

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:39.760
Youngblood, Reeves caught the world's attention
with River's Edge, a controversial movie about

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.960
apathetic teens and the killing of a young
girl.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.270
Soon, Reeves was showing up in way bigger
films — such as Dangerous Liaisons, which

00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.280
starred the likes of John Malkovich, Glenn
Close, Uma Thurman, and Michelle Pfieffer.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:57.200
Then, of course, he was cast as one of his
most iconic characters: the laid-back, time-traveling

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.960
wannabe rock star, Theodore Logan of Bill
&amp; Ted's Excellent Adventure.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.600
"Excellent!"

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:07.180
From there, he went on to prove his action
skills by catching waves and busting bank

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:08.140
robbers in Point Break.

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:13.180
Granted, Reeves also made a couple of missteps
early in his career — for example, he proved

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:17.480
to the world he's incapable of doing an English
accent with back-to-back performances in Bram

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:20.020
Stoker's Dracula and Much Ado About Nothing.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.230
But by this point, Reeves' career was speeding
forward nonetheless... and the actor was about

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:28.600
to make a huge leap from up-and-comer to blockbuster
king.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.960
The year 1994 was pretty phenomenal for film
in general, with the release of classics like

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:37.480
Pulp Fiction, The Shawshank Redemption, The
Lion King, and Forrest Gump.

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.360
Plus, adrenaline-junkies got their fix with
a whole slate of awesome action flicks such

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:47.810
as The Crow, True Lies, Leon: The Professional,
and of course, the movie that made Keanu Reeves

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:49.400
a bona fide star: Speed.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.700
Directed by Jan De Bont, Speed follows Jack
Traven, a police officer who finds himself

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:58.990
up against a psychopathic terrorist who's
rigged a bus to explode if it drops below

00:01:58.990 --> 00:01:59.900
50 mph.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:05.810
Along with courageous commuter Annie Porter,
Traven has to defuse the bomb, catch the bad

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:06.760
guy, and save the day.

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.160
Really, Speed is everything you could possibly
want from an action movie.

00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:15.560
Reeves and Bullock have a whole lot of sizzling
chemistry, the leading man did most of his

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.300
own stunts, and yeah, they really jumped that
bus.

00:02:19.170 --> 00:02:23.380
As a result, the movie impressed critics,
turned Reeves and Bullock into A-listers,

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.000
and earned $350 million against a $38 million
budget.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.680
Unfortunately, there's always a dark side
to success — and Speed's box office victory

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.520
was about to send Keanu Reeves to the cinematic
slammer.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.740
When a movie enjoys as much box office success
as Speed did, what happens next is pretty

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:40.260
much inevitable.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:45.660
And sure enough, the very week the money started
pouring in from Speed, Fox gave the go-ahead

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.320
for part two.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:51.370
As a result, director Jan De Bont started
reading new scripts and sounding out potential

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.440
ways for the story to go.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:57.680
Inspired by a recurring nightmare in which
a cruise ship crashed into an island, De Bont

00:02:57.681 --> 00:02:59.810
decided to set his sequel at sea.

00:02:59.810 --> 00:03:03.710
The movie would start off with Jack Traven
and Annie Porter as a married couple, with

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:08.069
the two going on a tropical cruise that is
rocked by a major terror attack, courtesy

00:03:08.069 --> 00:03:10.840
of a vengeful hacker who was fired by the
cruise line.

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:14.900
Of course, if you're going to make a Speed
sequel, you've got to bring back your original

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:15.440
stars.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:20.060
Bullock signed the dotted line after Fox promised
to finance Hope Floats, a romantic drama directed

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.140
by Forest Whitaker in which Bullock plays
a spurned housewife trying to take charge

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:24.820
of her life.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:30.220
But if you switch on Speed 2: Cruise Control
these days, you might notice that Keanu Reeves

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.120
is nowhere to be found.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:34.860
Instead, Jason Patric is playing some new
cop named Alex Shaw.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.740
So what's up with the switcheroo?

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.900
Why did Reeves say no to Speed 2?

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:44.860
Obviously, Fox wasn't happy when their star
abandoned ship, and the movie studio immediately

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:49.440
started spreading "propaganda" about him — saying
he was off touring with his rock band, Dogstar,

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:50.340
instead of making movies.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:53.980
According to Reeves, however, that couldn't
be further from the truth.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:58.660
There were several reasons he didn't want
to square off against Willem Dafoe's bug-eyed

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.860
terrorist, but playing the bass wasn't one
of them.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:05.020
So what did prevent Reeves from signing onto
the sequel?

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.500
Well, first off, he was all actioned out.

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:11.900
Reeves had just finished filming on Chain
Reaction, a thriller starring Morgan Freeman

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:12.700
and Rachel Weisz.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:17.920
The movie has Reeves starring as a scientist
who invents a new power source, only to find

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:20.740
himself framed for murder and on the run when
the FBI comes calling.

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.540
Critics weren't crazy about the action flick,
and neither was Reeves.

00:04:25.340 --> 00:04:29.940
He described the process of making the movie
as "really unsatisfying," lamenting the fact

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.720
that he had to do so much running and action.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:35.020
All in all, it was a disappointing experience
for Reeves.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:40.660
The experience left him exhausted and unwilling
to make Speed 2 so soon after working on another

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:41.200
action movie.

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.710
On top of all that, the star had also recently
broken his ankle in a motorcycle accident,

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:50.690
so doing all the stunts that would be required
of him for Cruise Control probably didn't

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:51.420
sound like much fun.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.800
Before casting Jason Patric as the lead in
Speed 2, Fox offered Keanu Reeves a hefty

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:59.740
paycheck of $11 million to reprise the role
of Jack Traven.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:02.220
But Reeves turned them down.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:06.130
Not just because he was tired of action films,
but also because he had his eye on new, more

00:05:06.130 --> 00:05:07.120
exciting projects.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:12.300
Reeves was particularly interested in playing
the lead role in the satanic legal thriller

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.220
The Devil's Advocate.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.800
This movie finds Reeves playing a hotshot
lawyer from Florida who's invited to join

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.680
a successful Manhattan law firm.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:24.740
When he shows up in the Big Apple, he meets
his new boss, a devilish wheeler and dealer

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.540
named John Milton, who seduces Reeves' character
into a life of sin, both in and out of the

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:30.060
courtroom.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:35.340
The Devil's Advocate was filming at the same
time as Speed 2: Cruise Control, but in Reeves'

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.560
mind, it was a pretty simple choice between
the two.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:42.600
He could act across from Al Pacino and scream
at Satan in a penthouse suite, or he could

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.700
get soaking wet doing aquatic stunts all day
long.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:50.100
Obviously, the choice was easy; better to
rule in Hell than swim in Speed 2.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.410
At the end of the day, however, there's one
reason above all that kept Reeves away from

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:56.620
Cruise Control: he thought the movie's script
absolutely sucked.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.860
In 2015, Reeves went on Jimmy Kimmel Live
to promote an upcoming film, and the talk

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:06.190
show host asked just why Reeves wasn't on
that cruise ship with poor Sandra Bullock.

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:06.960
Reeves answered honestly.

00:06:07.190 --> 00:06:11.500
"It was just a situation in life where I got
the script and I read the script and I was

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:12.320
like, ‘Argh!’”

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:15.260
Of course, trash-talking Speed 2 wasn't a
new look for Reeves.

00:06:16.020 --> 00:06:20.780
As far back as 1997, he was telling The Toronto
Star that "the script wasn't great."

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:24.340
So Reeves bowed out and let the Speed sequel
sail on by.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.919
And, as it turns out, he wasn't the only one
who thought Cruise Control was a really bad

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:29.460
idea.

00:06:30.200 --> 00:06:33.889
According to many, Speed 2 is the worst thing
that's happened to cruise ships since the

00:06:33.889 --> 00:06:35.600
Titanic plowed into that iceberg.

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:40.720
Hoping to recreate the success of the original
movie, Fox executives gave director Jan De

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:42.980
Bont a budget of $100 million for Speed 2
— which is $70 million more than the first

00:06:42.980 --> 00:06:43.440
film.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.580
But De Bont's Speed 2 still went over-budget,
with some suggesting the movie cost a whopping

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.200
$165 million to make.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:55.680
The studio poured a near-endless amount of
cash into this high-octane sequel, but nobody

00:06:55.680 --> 00:06:58.260
wanted a part two without their lovable leading
man.

00:06:58.900 --> 00:07:00.700
So Speed 2 bombed — hard.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.990
The movie made $164.5 million at the box office,
which is exactly what you don't want to happen

00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.880
when you've just made a movie with a budget
of $165 million.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:14.980
On top of that, the critics circled Speed
2's shipwreck like sharks, and wasted no time

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:15.620
in tearing it to pieces.

00:07:16.580 --> 00:07:21.340
Over on Rotten Tomatoes, the movie has a shockingly
low approval rating of just 4 percent.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:26.370
With Speed 2 sinking in theaters, it was suddenly
crystal clear that Keanu Reeves made the right

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:30.840
decision by staying far away from the doomed
sequel… but he wasn't to escape the disaster

00:07:30.840 --> 00:07:31.720
without a scratch.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:37.020
In the wake of Speed 2's disaster release,
20th Century Fox apparently decided it was

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.760
time to blacklist Keanu Reeves.

00:07:39.389 --> 00:07:44.240
Speaking with GQ in 2019, the actor claimed
that the company tossed him into what he called

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:48.430
"movie jail," banning him from starring in
any of their movies as retribution for turning

00:07:48.430 --> 00:07:49.380
down Cruise Control.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:54.430
Reeves spent 11 years on Fox's bad side, and
during that time, the studio released some

00:07:54.430 --> 00:07:58.620
pretty high profile films — such as X-Men,
The Phantom Menace, and Minority Report.

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:03.370
And you can't help but wonder how different
Reeves' career might look if the studio had

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:05.460
considered him for any one of those projects.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:12.120
Sadly, Fox didn't shine its spotlights his
way until 2008, when Reeves played an environmentally-conscious

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:13.800
alien in The Day the Earth Stood Still.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:18.740
And while he's not in the cinematic slammer
anymore, Reeves still doesn't do that many

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:20.160
studio films these days.

00:08:20.800 --> 00:08:24.320
The last time he worked with one of the big
companies for a live action, leading man role

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:29.170
was 47 Ronin with Universal Pictures, and
since then, he's taken to showing up in smaller

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:31.620
fare like The Bad Batch and The Neon Demon.

00:08:32.089 --> 00:08:36.600
Even the John Wick movies are relatively low-budget
affairs, distributed by the independent production

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:37.540
company Lionsgate.

00:08:38.209 --> 00:08:41.840
While we'll never know what movies Reeves
could have starred in had he remained in Fox's

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.290
good graces, the good news is that the actor's
career wasn't damaged too badly by the whole

00:08:46.290 --> 00:08:47.180
Speed 2 fiasco.

00:08:47.970 --> 00:08:51.900
Just because Fox didn't want to work with
Keanu Reeves, didn't mean the other studios

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:52.440
wouldn't, either.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:57.370
After all, he was a hot young actor with a
whole lot of promise, and in 1999, Reeves

00:08:57.370 --> 00:09:00.980
teamed up with Warner Brothers for a weird
little sci-fi flick called The Matrix.

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:06.880
With its epic gun battles, leather outfits,
and fascinating philosophical beliefs, the

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:10.820
movie landed with the impact of a well-placed
kung fu punch — and everybody started paying

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:12.820
serious attention to the man they called Neo.

00:09:13.660 --> 00:09:17.840
The Matrix revolutionized science fiction,
gave birth to two sequels and continues to

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:19.700
influence pop culture to this day.

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:22.640
In other words, Keanu Reeves was doing just
fine without Fox.

00:09:23.520 --> 00:09:27.490
And sure, there was a period post-Matrix where
Reeves wandered in the wilderness, making

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.840
a slew of subpar films like The Watcher, The
Lake House, and Street Kings.

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.680
But the guy was also making some interesting
choices too, with quirkier projects like Constantine

00:09:36.680 --> 00:09:37.460
and A Scanner Darkly.

00:09:37.920 --> 00:09:42.280
Of course, the Keanu Reeves renaissance really
began when a group of Russian gangsters broke

00:09:42.280 --> 00:09:44.060
into his home, killed his puppy, and stole
his car.

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:52.640
"People keep asking if I’m back and yeah,
I'm thinking I'm back."

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.720
John Wick was a sleeper hit that spawned a
franchise full of incredible fight choreography,

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:01.460
amazing worldbuilding, and some of Keanu Reeves'
greatest moments as an actor.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:06.459
Today, he's bigger than he's ever been, popping
up in huge movies such as Toy Story 4, making

00:10:06.459 --> 00:10:10.970
hilarious cameos in quirky comedies, showing
up at conventions, and receiving all kinds

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:12.300
of adoration on the internet.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:15.300
All that, and he never did have to do Speed
2.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:16.580
Could have been worse.

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.060
